#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cba19aec-6df6-5aab-9bad-096cc0f2cd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/6b48a66edce1084ebb52340009ae3db3abce37166b057266e66c222ae5a23252.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330276} {:answer/id #uuid "776b3c5f-2284-5c22-9efb-6ab1a09b9cc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/8cabfc56ca77993d6cf0c03aca24e9dc027ff933c0bd3714aac27ec974c95c25.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330277} {:answer/id #uuid "e49c0ff9-2c18-5dcb-99d9-671ab65c5073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/5d0ba5d7d6f0367c80cbfeba7e7883a0476b64c04ca31506baa84130743b3f4e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330278} {:answer/id #uuid "7a853bd7-ac51-5895-916d-e719c50af152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/e45e237be0e30847cbbabad7c84a56e7d155b7f69d4ea38be37bc6895798d9fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330279} {:answer/id #uuid "54504149-c796-59d2-a6cf-43ba556bbd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/9496ea429c904ca49700fca369fe60d0237c48a91a76a09f6636835d92f9b416.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54504149-c796-59d2-a6cf-43ba556bbd2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/9496ea429c904ca49700fca369fe60d0237c48a91a76a09f6636835d92f9b416.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330280} :answer-field/id #uuid "592db1a4-5afb-55cc-a7cc-c2f2533a1438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89d7879e-efea-5b0c-9087-4e16cff8d558" :question/math-academy-id "q-121268" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x-1)(y-2).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/2ae1b749a56deaef05254ff376acbd44885337b06b49473cb9b19b4fa57f6c84.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/6434bee1a3dc38292675749072f046d4af6239ce6a8f71f102a9b29637679da4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-1)$ | $(y-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(0,3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(2,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(0,1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)>0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/17551c745895bac085fc289f9ff0f26531bc518891815e252885fd75d81a0af0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce6b38c9-84cb-5618-875d-54dba929f081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330283} {:answer/id #uuid "4202c560-65d8-5a16-b7b8-d406d803752a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330284} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6c4794-75ed-59a5-82d8-9c2ff6d361be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330285} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c4fff8-c863-5411-aefa-d995f8ca046a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330286} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd98aa4-a892-5099-ba7e-e6ee0c0ca112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa6c4794-75ed-59a5-82d8-9c2ff6d361be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330285} :answer-field/id #uuid "1face21b-2b63-54e6-9b37-962321d8d5c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9195bf5b-e185-53ad-970f-942b816b9aec" :question/math-academy-id "q-121278" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=(x+1)(2-y).$ What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y),$ shown below?\n\n| $y>2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/d5d7c89d619aa13d7fea8bc1260a08cb8678fb260e4431508c5f4c8c53e7ba5e.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/6cce64627f21f6cc17efc789614a0bd582ded827a0938f01c9b756e0cb461862.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+1)$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(0,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(0,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/c24cc3ab0812bfae6834ff8c3a68668c3925f6e39e29a453ea6bc27fbc9f6c74.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the table as follows:\n\n| $y>2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c731b5cb-a6b0-5a00-8688-55d70711591f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/97e1785d876935df14a9d7cea236f536c90b68fa1bad2bf8418569c80e56ba12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330290} {:answer/id #uuid "34e8a91c-2fbe-5b32-bd99-a19b88fdaa7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/0ba31669fe6eb9d3eadd9423de52ea1042300fdc098cb3e9bd8081ed458c0d07.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330291} {:answer/id #uuid "26a986b6-012e-5094-a812-6714e976e5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/f12e4490704485f3b05921fe9e25b21bc04aa389995ec2e4580593f63883e47c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330292} {:answer/id #uuid "12a669e5-6040-56a5-890c-55593b954e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/8a9becf0529b2896627d44fef11066d684c50479e62166ccc777a6250cd24033.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330293} {:answer/id #uuid "89dce387-0459-545e-9037-a100dad3d10c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/f0614c91d93dc23135aad96e908562cfe1f6726f1c2b641f0ee55952deb4625b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c731b5cb-a6b0-5a00-8688-55d70711591f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/97e1785d876935df14a9d7cea236f536c90b68fa1bad2bf8418569c80e56ba12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330290} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce98d6f6-0564-515b-b6de-c7adfea9fc7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5272563d-3782-5aa6-b37c-de65aac06d7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149547" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x+2){y}^{3}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\ge 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-2$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/975b596621dab7d455b09d75b1c8d2b9fd73e75cd2169ec40aadda48673f6883.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/9466a3fbf682ce967cda2294cfb61d5fa40b4f7c91686cfc8653cb4c1ffb4e97.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+2)$ | ${y}^{3}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-3,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-3,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/b8e5807115c13aa842364a174058d1081bd84eba10dce13390da93832a1aeb54.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b727039b-016b-5dcd-a554-589bc1eb5dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/f0547f4bf2530cbcc271e81ba501c3710b0359bb222d1e56014acac6675b515d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330297} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb86582-4a82-5cc1-a06f-708fd5a7166c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/cd0a8472d9b1abff7090be8547c654f8842b6fc934f768d8c34fe208e4058990.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330298} {:answer/id #uuid "9cad1796-5cb7-5981-b480-161d04187ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/2f661772948f506a7fa8f0a205cf2e84b3a10dfc9bc35b695d572755ca35c917.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330299} {:answer/id #uuid "39bafa95-736b-5e31-ba87-57e44397aae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/1cadc33591f95b5b66f50794e329768364708bb7f9de1e06d579ff1833be11d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330300} {:answer/id #uuid "be469f9c-054d-55d1-b764-7ba7fd34b7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/7c1521cfad08f4c1cc4a699066ec5dc84fd635c2f1d39f71dca5a8ba86533887.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bb86582-4a82-5cc1-a06f-708fd5a7166c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/cd0a8472d9b1abff7090be8547c654f8842b6fc934f768d8c34fe208e4058990.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330298} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ad0811-692c-57bd-90a9-0db23570a47d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3557c58-b79b-5ef1-8132-f7d04ee3b456" :question/math-academy-id "q-149550" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(3-x){y}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=3$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/e4d289aef282e076b062aab6d8f0f209396313e2c6403b2e27d1c0272ac90795.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/4cef3532a8e70098c030f2341800f06e9e54e4074126a0a97aecdf1f1c5185d0.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | ${y}^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/c9ed172c5746b1b0afd2d9b7b3bbb21245115df164bfb7e34f4f530887376e28.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b47fd2f-14a8-5532-95c1-d4cff81e403a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330304} {:answer/id #uuid "6801b55f-6b3c-5b5a-b4aa-426ef1df51b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b47fd2f-14a8-5532-95c1-d4cff81e403a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 1